Chapter: SmpDispatch

 An application processor claims a process out of the process table and
 runs it.

 Six children are spawned and each spins for a while before exiting. The
 boot processor takes one; every other core is in the same dispatch loop
 and takes one too. Each claim made by a core whose id is not zero adds
 one to cell 36200 with a locked add, and the boot processor's id is
 always zero.

 Nothing else writes that cell. The host does not touch it and no path the
 boot processor can take reaches it. So a value above zero is not evidence
 that six processes ran -- it is evidence that a core which is not the boot
 processor took one out of the table and executed it. A probe that only
 showed six children finishing would pass exactly as happily on one core,
 and a test that cannot fail is how SMP came to be faked here once already.

 The spin is long on purpose. An idle core now halts rather than busy-scans,
 and rises again on a wake IPI or its timer, so it takes a millisecond or so
 to claim newly published work -- during which the never-halting boot
 processor, arriving in the dispatch loop the instant it blocks, can run a
 short child to completion itself. If each child finished in less than that
 wake latency the boot processor would take all six and no application
 processor would claim any, and the test would report NO AP RAN A PROCESS
 while dispatch worked perfectly. The child outlives the wake by a wide
 margin, so a core that wakes has work still waiting. This is the same
 reason smp-preempt spins long.

Section: Body

  spin : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spin (n) (acc) = if n == 0 then acc else spin (n - 1) (acc + 1)

  child-body : Integer -> Integer
  child-body (x) = spin 100000000 0

  opening : [Console, Concurrent] Nothing = act
    a <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    b <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    c <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    d <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    e <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    f <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wa <- process-wait a
    wb <- process-wait b
    wc <- process-wait c
    wd <- process-wait d
    we <- process-wait e
    wf <- process-wait f
    print-line-uni ("aps checked in: " & show (atomic-load 4080))
    print-line-uni (if atomic-load 36200 > 0
                    then "an ap claimed and ran a process"
                    else "NO AP RAN A PROCESS")
  end
